238525390625, -0.03253173828125, -0.016082763671875, -0.045684814453125, 0.01337432861328125, 0.0202789306640625, -0.006717681884765625, 0.0191650390625, 0.02093505859375, -0.0157470703125, -0.01560211181640625, 0.0302734375, 0.00958251953125, 0.03643798828125, -0.0025653839111328125, 0.006961822509765625, -0.008575439453125, 0.0384521484375, 0.0090484619140625, 0.0010461807250976562, 0.0277252197265625, -0.0290679931640625, -0.01259613037109375, 0.004558563232421875, -0.018310546875, -0.01611328125, 0.025360107421875, 0.0028533935546875, 0.03594970703125, -0.02447509765625, 0.001384735107421875, -0.002735137939453125, 0.0028839111328125, 0.023773193359375, -0.0176239013671875, 0.0025463104248046875, -0.024810791015625, -0.02777099609375, 0.043182373046875, -0.01898193359375, -0.0132293701171875, 0.0159149169921875, 0.0057830810546875, 0.02569580078125, 0.0174560546875, 0.046356201171875, 0.043426513671875, -0.01090240478515625, -0.00942230224609375, -0.004596710205078125], "payload": {"model": "text-embedding-3-small", "text": "Purpose: To develop and assess a locally deployed multi-agent AI system for structuring radiology reports and providing quality assurance.\n\nMaterials and Methods: In this retrospective study, 638 radiology reports from CT examinations of the chest, abdomen, and pelvis\u2014dictated by 15 board-certified radiologists in 2023 and 2024\u2014were analyzed. A multi-agent AI pipeline was created to perform report structuring and quality assurance (QA). The system organized reports into standardized anatomical sections at the sentence level using a combination of regular expression rules and locally hosted large language models. It also detected mismatches between the Findings and Impression sections or within sections, gender-anatomy inconsistencies, and undocumented communication of critical findings. Two board-certified radiologists independently reviewed a subset of 45 reports.\n\nResults: The multi-agent system structured the Findings sections of all reports (22,270 sentences) into a predefined anatomical format while preserving the original content. The system flagged 90 reports (14.1%), most commonly for section mismatches (80 reports, 12.5%). In the radiologist evaluation, both reviewers agreed that 31 reports (69%) were correctly restructured, 2 reports (4%) were incorrectly restructured, and disagreed on 12 reports (27%). Both reviewers agreed that no clinically important information was omitted and no fabricated content was introduced. Overall QA performance was rated as \"excellent\" or \"good\" in 84% of evaluated reports, with the remainder rated as \"fair.\"\n\nConclusion: A locally deployed multi-agent AI system effectively integrated radiology report structuring and quality assurance into a unified workflow. The system demonstrated favorable performance in radiologist evaluation and may support standardization and quality assurance in radiology practice."}}